      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "Several of these comments in this bug report could be attributed to (well-known) Intel I225-V/I226-V/Realtek RTL8126 hardware bugs with Energy Efficient Ethernet (EEE) or Realtek RTL8125 bugs with ASPM. Manufacturers have worked around the hardware bugs from NIC factory by disabling EEE by default in a firmware update or in a kernel driver.\n\nSome of these comments respondents have said their issues to be solved by a firmware update, a hardware replacement of a buggy NIC, or network switch changes where implicitly a newly changed switch either doesn't support EEE or disables the feature by default.\n\nI'm aware of similar EEE issues which may generally manifest with e.g. single TCP stream connections being throttled or dropping packets, but not with multiple parallel TCP stream connections to an IP-address (replicated on a Cisco Catalyst C1000 switch while EEE was enabled by default, but not on a dumb cheap switch). UDP seemed to be less affected by this on Intel I225-V/I226-V NICs.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
